# Backstop only. package.json "files" is what actually decides the tarball:
# dist/ minus its source maps, plus README.md, CHANGELOG.md and LICENSE.
#
# NOT docs/ — this line used to claim "publish only dist + docs", which was never
# true. docs/ is not in "files" and was correctly never shipped: it holds the
# rendered example GIFs, and docs/demo.gif alone is 3.4 MB against a ~130 KB
# package. They belong in the repo and behind the README's image URLs, not in
# every install.
#
# NOT the source maps either. Every dist/**/*.js.map and *.d.ts.map lists
# `sources: ["../src/*.ts"]` with no `sourcesContent`, and src/ is deliberately
# not published — so each shipped map resolved to a file that was not there and
# debugged nothing, for 152 KB and 34% of the unpacked tarball. Emitting
# `sourcesContent` instead would put the whole TypeScript source inside the maps,
# which is larger than shipping src/ outright and still slower to load.
#
# Excluding them from the tarball was only half the job, and the half that
# leaves no trace. The build still EMITTED maps, so every shipped .js and .d.ts
# ended with `//# sourceMappingURL=…` pointing at a file that was no longer in
# the package: 78 of them. Node ignores that silently; bundlers and
# source-map-loader warn once per file, and an editor asked to follow a
# declaration map finds nothing. So `tsc -p tsconfig.json` — the build that
# ships — emits no maps at all and therefore no comments, and the two lines
# below stay as a backstop for a dist/ left over from a dev build. Local
# development keeps its maps: `npm run watch` and `npm run build:dev` use
# tsconfig.dev.json, where the sources they point at do exist.
src/
examples/
docs/
.github/
tsconfig.json
tsconfig.dev.json
*.tsbuildinfo
.gifsmith/
